#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180565} :activity/id #uuid "1c5ce268-312c-52a6-9777-1ff390251f96" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048498 :topic/id #uuid "a459a1e3-e019-5c4e-a702-5c6685ca3dec" :topic/math-academy-id 3707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180763 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is an affine transformation. The **inverse** of $\\mathbf T$ (if it exists) is an affine transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\\mathbf T^{-1} (\\mathbf T(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf T (\\mathbf T^{-1}(\\mathbf u)) = \\mathbf u$$\n\nfor every vector $\\mathbf u\\in\\mathbb R^2.$ In other words, the transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ reverses the action of $\\mathbf T.$\n\nNot all affine transformations are invertible. So how can we determine whether a given affine transformation is invertible?\n\nTo answer this, recall that an affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ can be expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. We can reverse the action of $\\mathbf T$ by making $\\mathbf u$ the subject of this equation, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ {A}^{-1}\\cdot Au & ={A}^{-1}\\cdot (x-b) \\\\ {I}_{2}\\cdot u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b) \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis equation gives us a method of computing the inverse of an affine transformation. Additionally, we have the following:\n\n*An affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is invertible if and only if the matrix $A$ that represents the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is invertible, that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf99caa7-1e47-5fea-87ac-9b91151af127" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7697 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35dfb0c0-efab-5193-92eb-6eb22cc2af9f" :step/math-academy-id 44660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179437 :question/id #uuid "f9d3291b-b95b-536b-93ec-9815f7f554d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12428"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e39ae7fc-ff0a-5b97-8e13-1f15b12a07e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049554}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378510}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Invertible Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "30275202-8cd8-51e0-a9e1-92aa287dbe42" :step/math-academy-id 44661 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180764 :tutorial/content "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ expressed in matrix form as\n\n$$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf x} = \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{A} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf u} + \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{\\mathbf b}.$$\n\nWe wish to determine whether $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ exists and find its matrix form if it does exist.\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get $$\\det(A) = \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1\\cdot (-2) - (-3) \\cdot 1 = 1 \\neq 0.$$\n\nSince $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTo compute the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ we will use the formula we derived earlier:\n\n$$\\mathbf{u} = A^{-1}(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$$\n\nWe start by calculating the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix form of the inverse affine transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4c88a3e-6514-5b56-ab23-306164b0a3a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7698 :tutorial/title "Finding the Inverse of an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "6802d261-21db-5122-886a-1dda8f3a0db4" :step/math-academy-id 44664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179438 :question/id #uuid "af48b8ba-874a-5753-9874-a2b13fc5a65b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0403251a-9491-5d04-ab5a-ed027024d0a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050950}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378524}] :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "3f8aecda-4d54-5528-939f-886f4ab93593" :step/math-academy-id 44665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180569 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179439 :question/id #uuid "c5e70edd-5a39-5e85-9461-68df182780dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378531}] :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"} :step/id #uuid "21d1cffb-5a12-5ee7-ad2a-181e2a8d4474" :step/math-academy-id 44668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179442 :question/id #uuid "98ebb2b6-475b-53c5-acae-0b1b0f0778d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12441"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049260}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389263}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "35abe35f-9e29-539b-aaa5-a2546fd2fbfb" :step/math-academy-id 44704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180767 :tutorial/content "Consider the square in the $uv$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8cc0923da86a1a1ac6b666893397263ef3865448080500caa6f17510547d901.png)\n\nWe wish to explore the image of this square under an affine transformation $$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},$$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors.\n\nSimilar to linear transformations, there are three possibilities depending on the value of $\\det(A)$.\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular ($\\det(A)\\neq 0$). In this case, our square is mapped to a parallelogram.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane, we get the following parallelogram:\n\n![](images/36/36da067393fa53f4296373d65e3c6177a54d1a901824188687b4a74ea171afdf.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ has at least one nonzero entry. In this case, our square is mapped to a line segment.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following line segment:\n\n![](images/5e/5e5481e5b2f2218a6097020e260dc991cde05574372c724aa07527458257d94c.png)\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular ($\\det(A)=0$) and $A$ is the zero matrix. In this case, our square is mapped to a point on the plane.\n\nFor example, let the transformation $\\mathbf T$ be given by $$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] + [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$$ Mapping our square to the $xy$-plane gives the following point.\n\n![](images/00/0072c5e06674f79f82e771744fda217e6558830f7a6e4ab387aa9dbf4dda09bc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "375d6591-08dd-5dbf-b36a-28013839a28b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7706 :tutorial/title "Image Types Formed by Affine Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "5ec4662b-6870-532f-83f9-f7a9cbe5504e" :step/math-academy-id 44705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180768 :tutorial/content "In general, if we consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by the mapping\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, then we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases: \n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane (namely, the point with position vector $\\mathbf b$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "560907bc-354a-5693-a8df-e6824454755d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7710 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Image Types"} :step/id #uuid "d71cf118-7383-5aea-a47c-8359fc7dffe4" :step/math-academy-id 44723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180574}}] :activity/title "The Inverse of an Affine Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179866}]}
